we often talk about the economy of the  United States and we have a lot of  different questions that we might ask  about the economy but one common one is  what indicators can we look at to tell  us whether the economy is doing well  right now and there's a lot of debate  over what the right answer to that  question is and number two there's a  question that is more forward-looking  which is are there any indicators that  can give us a clue about how the economy  might do in the future and in general  I've said before and the data backs up  that we're generally not very good at  predicting where the economy is going to  go we can sort of generally say that at  a certain point in the next number of  years there's going to be an economic  downturn and we can say that with some  certitude because it tends to always  happen if you look back in history at a  certain point there's always some kind  of downturn but our ability to predict  exactly when it will be is relatively  poor economists aren't particularly good  at it everybody's pretty bad at it  financial advisors aren't particularly  good at it but that doesn't mean there  aren't indicators we can look at that  can provide at least an early warning  sign that certain aspects of the economy  are starting to not look quite so  positive and one important indicator  might not be one you've heard of before  is our people able to afford their car  payments and we have new data which  tells us that a record seven million  Americans are 90 days or more behind on  their car loan payments three months or  more behind and this is a number that's  higher than during the 2008 financial  crisis even adjusted for the population  growth that the country has seen in the  ten years since so a reasonable question  would be okay fine David a record number  of people or thirty are three months  behind on car payments but why does that  matter the reason it matters is that car  payments are a priority for most people  that have a car loan and the reason why  is pretty obvious if you have a car you  probably need the car to either get to  work which allows you to earn money to  pay for your other expenses  or to get to school or to drive your  kids around so for many Americans their  car payment is a top-priority payment  every single month and losing that car  if it becomes repossessed means you  might not be able to go to work which  means you will eventually not be able to  make your rent or mortgage payments and  you can sort of imagine how it has this  cascading negative effect now there are  lots of problems in the auto loan  industry that we can point to there are  more and more of these subprime  financing options which are letting  people buy cars that are way more  expensive than their budgets really  dictate they should be driving there are  record long car loans 84 months I've  even heard of longer loans than that  these are insanely long loans on assets  that depreciate cars depreciate very  quickly particularly in their first few  years you probably should be buying a  cheaper car before you get a car loan  that is 84 months long  predatory rates where people pay  astronomically high interest rates on  their car loans terrible public transit  pushes people to get cars who other my  otherwise might say you know I can just  take public transit to get to work or to  school so there are all these problems  in the car lending industry but that  doesn't change that it's a really bad  sign that a record number of Americans  are three months behind on their car  loans and I don't say this because I'm  worried about automobile manufacturers I  don't say this because I'm worried that  auto lending companies are going to be  affected or that it's bad for the travel  industry if people lose their cars and  won't take road trips or whatever this  is bad because think about the context  we just had quote tax cuts from Donald  Trump and Republicans we have an  exploding deficit we have cheap gas we  have a low federal funds rate we have  historically low unemployment in terms  of the rate we have the stock market  near all-time highs and yet we still  have a record number of people who are  unable to make their car payments and  this relates to broader problems in the  economy cost of living is one  stagnant wages is another health care  spending by individuals and families is  another student loan debt the cost of  education and so when we look at this  indicator we have to realize it's not  about is this going to hurt GM it's more  about why is it that with so many  economic indicators actually looking  fine are we still seeing this degree of  late payments on auto loans and there's  a couple other notable things for people  kind of interested in the car loan  industry there's this common defense  that it's not that problematic for  people to default on car loans because  the car loans are collateralized what  this means is that when a bank gives you  a car loan you were giving them the car  as collateral meaning if you don't pay  the loan or you default on the loan as  it is more often said the lender can  just repossess the car and sell it and  they're fine cars depreciate rapidly so  the idea that that's not a problem is  simply not true yes the car gets  repossessed but the car is worth very  little compared to the original purchase  price number two lenders are more and  more willing to extend loans to people  who probably shouldn't be buying cars  that are that expensive because with GPS  tracking technology lenders are also  more secure in knowing that if the  person defaults they can use the GPS  tracker to just find the car and get it  back that's also a real problem in the  car lending industry that is extending  credit once again to people who should  be having much smaller loans rather than  extending loans over seventy to eighty  four or in some cases I've even heard of  ninety six-month car loans this is the  double-edged sword sort of of technology  but not a good indicator and it is one  that a lot of economists are paying very  very close attention to does this mean  we can predict exactly when the next  downturn is going to be no it doesn't  but it's an important indicator to look  at
 

